With a positive confirmation from the editor test, you can move on to the next phase!

### Launch to Testing Edition

Here, we’ll put together a focus group to use the new version of the agent and log feedback on its function, output quality and overall usability. Since the sandbox is only visible to the EM/GA workspace, they must use the testing edition of your agent for this phase. This document contains a sample of the testing release email you can send to the pilot group. Again, include a link to the correct version of the agent for easy bookmarking, and be sure to specify the desired duration of the test.

**Important note:** Some agents may not have a testing edition, as these releases are required only once a build is in use by the team and changes to it would cause disruption. If the agent you’re updating doesn’t have one yet, you’ll need to create one per the steps outlined above in the New Build Testing section.

As described in that section, this part of the process is a bit of a waiting game as the pilot group evaluates and reports on the updates. Once the test parameters have been fulfilled, you can check the sandbox back in and move on to the launch.

### Take the Updates Live

Various documents will need to be created and updated before we can formally launch and announce an agent update. Again, the agent update protocol template is going to be integral in guiding you through here.

You’ll also need to review the communication templates in this document to determine which apply to your situation, and then make sure you update the rest of the team. Ideally, we want to time launches around Copy Squad Write Clubs to streamline information delivery. You can see the date of the next Write Club by checking the Copy Team calendar.

When it’s time to complete the process and unleash your creation upon the world, **make sure you link to the newest version of the agent so it’s crystal clear which one team members should use**. Since the testing edition was the proving ground for the live agent, there should theoretically be no harm in someone continuing to use it by accident, but again, clear is kind, and we want to minimize confusion wherever possible.
